let __v_866 = Value::from("An antacid or an alginate (Gaviscon and the like) settles most heartburn within minutes, and a pharmacy tablet such as omeprazole, taken daily for a fortnight, works better if it keeps coming back. Eat smaller meals, leave three hours between dinner and bed, and raise the head of the bed 10 to 15cm. See a GP if it lasts more than three weeks or you find food sticking on the way down.");
frame.store("_HEARTBURN_ANSWER", __v_866.clone())?;
let __v_867 = Value::from("Cut {} out for a fortnight and see if the heartburn eases; an antacid or an alginate (Gaviscon and the like) settles a bad night in minutes. Tomato, chilli, onion and garlic, citrus, fried food, chocolate, mint, coffee and alcohol are the other usual triggers, so smaller meals, three hours between dinner and bed and the head of the bed 10 to 15cm up do the rest. See a GP if it lasts more than three weeks.");
frame.store("_HEARTBURN_BLAMED", __v_867.clone())?;
let __v_868 = Value::from("Heartburn is very common in pregnancy and eases once the baby is born: the hormones relax the valve at the top of the stomach, and later the baby takes the room. Eat little and often, stay upright an hour after eating, prop the head of the bed up 10 to 15cm, and try an antacid or an alginate (Gaviscon and the like) - ask a pharmacist or your midwife before omeprazole. Tell your midwife if it stops you eating or sleeping.");
frame.store("_HEARTBURN_PREGNANT", __v_868.clone())?;
let __v_869 = Value::from("Some painkillers set heartburn off - ibuprofen, aspirin and naproxen are the usual three, because they irritate the stomach lining. Do not just stop a tablet you were prescribed: ask a pharmacist or your GP whether paracetamol would do instead, and take any of them with food. An antacid or an alginate settles a bad night in the meantime, and see a GP if it lasts more than three weeks.");
frame.store("_HEARTBURN_PILLS", __v_869.clone())?;
let __v_870 = Value::from("Plain, dry food sits easiest: porridge, rice, bread, bananas, melon, ginger, plain chicken or fish, and a glass of milk - little and often beats one big meal, and nothing within three hours of bed. Go steady on tomato, chilli, onion and garlic, citrus, fried and fatty food, chocolate, mint, coffee and alcohol, since those are the usual triggers. An antacid or an alginate (Gaviscon and the like) settles a bad night in minutes.");
frame.store("_HEARTBURN_EAT", __v_870.clone())?;
